Correction to my opening report: I checked two pinned revisions of PR #7960 today. The initial revision (`0cd67042`) executed for six unknown verdicts: `denied`, `DENY`, `block`, `error`, `""`, and `None`. The contributor hardened that same PR in `ec6cf48c` on September 14, before I opened this issue. All six now block, and the hardened revision validates the action hash too. The sample is still in an open PR, not in AutoGen main. I should have checked the latest PR head before filing.

I also got one example wrong in the issue body: exact `"deny"` blocked even in the initial revision. The claim that 15 of 36 projects currently have this defect is withdrawn; the audit mixed revisions and is not a current prevalence estimate. I moved this case out of the active list and published the old-versus-new comparison and runnable source-pinned check: https://github.com/arian-gogani/failopen/tree/main/historical/autogen-verdict

@sattyamjjain, yes, the repository is MIT licensed, so you may use the historical case with the required license notice and attribution. Please label it as a regression pattern, not an unresolved AutoGen vulnerability. A static rule should catch an untrusted verdict flowing through exact-string checks into an execution fallthrough. The current PR head is a useful negative test: it must not trigger on the explicit non-permit branch.

